A||N||9||Seanh||Removal of ball joints, contol arms, coil springs?||||1||
Z||000000||Seanh||06-22-2001||01:19 PM||||I got my Fiero Store order today, of new front end suspension bushings, ball joints, all that wonderful stuff! I also ordered Rodney's ball joint seperator to remove them. Now, tonight I am going to get started, after work, taking them all off. I want to remove everything possible, so I can paint and clean it all up. I bought the bushing set for the front contol arms, so they both must come off. Iv read thru, and heard of many different methods of removeing the coil springs. So, hows the best and easiest way to do it? Also, how is the best way to put the springs back in? They are stock height. Which contol arm should I remove first? Anything else that I should know that would be helpful before I start? Thanks!||||reg||1
Z||000001||Leadfoot||06-22-2001||01:41 PM||||You might need a cold chisel and a torch to snap off the ends of the bolts that go through the control arm bushings.  The often rust to the sleeve and are a b!@#% to remove.  Also, be sure to spray your sway bar bolts and rack and pinion bolts with penetrating oil so they don't snap as well.  You will have to remove the rack an pinion to get to the lower front control arm bolt.  Good luck!||||reg||1
Z||000002||Seanh||06-22-2001||01:44 PM||||I guess I should have said that as well. I have the rack & pinion, sway bar, brakes, braces off. Already broke 3 of the 4 sway bar bolts, even after spraying with oil.||||reg||1
Z||000003||Seanh||06-22-2001||02:09 PM||||Somthing just occured to me. I'm using Rodney's ball joint seperator, not one of the pickle fork looking ones. So, don't I have to seperate both ball joints at the same time right? His instructions say to just remove the cotter pins from both nuts, and loosen the nut 1 turn. Support the lower control arm with a floor jack, giving it 1" of clearance. Then insert tool and twist until both ball joints seperate. Then what would I do since I'm wanting to remove both control arms and coil springs? Thanks||||reg||1
Z||000004||starfighter007@msn.com||06-22-2001||05:49 PM||||you should just go to auto zone and get a pickle fork they may even have one you could rent,either way there not very expensive maybe $15.00 or so I am not sure I know what this tool is that you got from Mr. Dickman is.boy it sounds like that front end is really gonna look pretty when your done with it. good luck ||||reg||1
Z||000005||Seanh||06-22-2001||06:07 PM||||Rodney's tool, Iv heard, its a little safer and easier to use then a pickle fork thing. I have always wanted to do the front suspension on my car, and this gave a good opportunity to do so. Kinda pissed me off that it came at such as inconvient time, but if it didn't happen, I probally wouldn't have done it any time soon. Can't wait to get started after work today.||||reg||1
Z||000006||Leadfoot||06-22-2001||06:14 PM||||Seanh,<BR>I forgot to tell you that the best way to remove the spring is to let the jack all the way down (after you removed the knuckle) and to use a prybar at the bottom to nudge it out.  You might want to secure the spring with rope to the crossmember just in case it pops out suddenly.  To put it back in I just used my prybar again and my right foot (hence leadfoot) to kick the bottom of the spring onto the lower control arm.  It may sound funny but it worked.  You are really gonna have to man handle it though.  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/biggrin.gif"> I replaced all my pushings and the driveability improved 100%! <BR>||||reg||1
Z||000007||Seanh||06-22-2001||06:24 PM||||Alright, I think I'm understanding what I should do now. <P>Support lower control arm with floor jack<BR>Loosen the upper and lower are pivot bolts<BR>Remove the cotter pin, and back off the nut 1 turn<BR>Insert the ball joint seperator and serperate both joints<BR>Secure coil spring, and slowly let the lower arm down<BR>Remove coil spring<BR>Remove control arm pivot bolts<BR>Remove control arms<P>Sound about right?||||reg||1
Z||000008||Seanh||06-22-2001||06:31 PM||||One other thing, as far as re-installing everything. Is there anyway to put the spring back in *easier*? I am going to have everything painted and all, so I'd like not to damage the paint job on installation if thats possible. Oh well, I can repaint once installed if I have to I guess.||||reg||1
Z||000009||Leadfoot||06-22-2001||06:51 PM||||You got it!  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/wink.gif">  As far as putting the spring in, you might want to rent a spring compressor tool.  I had one laying around but it was too big to get in such a small space.  Other than that I don't know what else you can do.  Try getting a friend to help you put the spring in.  I only kicked the spring in because I am wimp  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/biggrin.gif"> (not).<P>||||reg||1
